  "keyInsight": "Heat flows only during the two isothermal steps (Qh in at Th, Qc out at Tc) while the insulated adiabatic steps merely shuttle the gas between those two temperatures — the surroundings block under the cylinder, not the piston motion, tells you whether heat can flow.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Faithful four-panel reconstruction of the source figure only — no p-V diagram, no invented curves or constants. Four vertical panels in a row, each a tall colored block with a centered white cylinder cut into it. Block fills must be saturated: a strong warm red for the hot reservoir (step 1) and a strong cool blue for the cold reservoir (step 3), not washed pastels; insulation blocks (steps 2 and 4) a pale neutral beige. Block labels ('Reservoir' / 'Insulation', plus '(Temperature Th)' / '(Temperature Tc)' for reservoirs) are centered horizontally at the bottom of each block, with enough bottom padding that no text is clipped — verify the step-4 'Insulation' label renders in full. Gas is light gray with a dense dot stipple; the piston is a darker gray horizontal cap on top of the gas column with only a short stubby handle tab protruding upward — not a long rod spanning the cylinder. Heat arrows thick and gray (Qh up into the gas in step 1, Qc down out of it in step 3); work arrows thinner and green beside the pistons (W1, W2 up; W3, W4 down). Gas column heights rank step 2 ≈ step 3 (tallest) > step 1 > step 4 (shortest). Bold black captions centered under each panel: step number and process name, then the (X→Y) transition. The stepState button highlights the active panel (full saturation, emphasized arrows) and dims the others. The thermalContact toggle must NOT change stepState: it only emphasizes which panels share the selected boundary type (e.g. 'Isothermal' highlights panels 1 and 3 with a colored outline) while the currently selected step stays selected."
}

